---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc853.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope — the Two Control Indicators" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Excluded Arrangements" },
        { id: "s4", label: "The Intangible Asset Model" },
        { id: "s5", label: "The Receivable Model" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Revenue Recognition During the Construction/Upgrade Phase" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — the Intangible Asset Model (a Toll Road)" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — the Receivable Model (Guaranteed Availability Payments)" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "ASC 853 vs IFRIC 12 — a Genuinely Converged Standard" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci"><div class="ctitle ci">Why the operator doesn't recognise the infrastructure as its own</div><div>In a SERVICE CONCESSION ARRANGEMENT, a private OPERATOR builds, upgrades, operates, and/or maintains PUBLIC infrastructure (e.g. a toll road, water treatment plant, or airport) on behalf of a public-sector GRANTOR — but because the GRANTOR retains control over the infrastructure and its residual interest, the operator does NOT recognise the infrastructure as its own PROPERTY, PLANT &amp; EQUIPMENT. Instead, ASC 853 requires the operator to recognise either an INTANGIBLE ASSET or a RECEIVABLE, depending on the arrangement's payment structure.</div></div>
    <div class="card cs"><div class="ctitle cs">A standard added specifically to converge with IFRIC 12</div><div>ASC 853 was added to the Codification SPECIFICALLY to align US GAAP with IFRIC 12 (Service Concession Arrangements) — making this, like ASC 606/IFRS 15 and ASC 820/IFRS 13, a genuinely CONVERGED area by deliberate design, rather than a coincidental similarity.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — the Two Control Indicators</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Both indicators generally must be present</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>Indicator</span><span>Description</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>1</span><span>The GRANTOR controls or regulates WHAT services the operator must provide with the infrastructure, TO WHOM it must provide them, and AT WHAT PRICE</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.4fr 2.6fr;"><span>2</span><span>The GRANTOR controls — through ownership, beneficial entitlement, or otherwise — any SIGNIFICANT RESIDUAL INTEREST in the infrastructure at the END of the arrangement</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Excluded Arrangements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Genuine leases and operator-retained residual interest fall outside scope</div><div>Arrangements are EXCLUDED from ASC 853 if they meet the definition of a LEASE (ASC 842 applies instead), or if the OPERATOR (rather than the grantor) retains a SUBSTANTIVE residual interest in the infrastructure at the arrangement's end — in that case, normal PP&amp;E accounting under ASC 360 would generally apply instead.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>The Intangible Asset Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Used when the operator bears demand risk</div><div>Where the operator's right is to CHARGE USERS of the public service directly (e.g. toll road users pay tolls), and the AMOUNT of consideration is CONTINGENT on the extent that the public actually USES the service (genuine DEMAND RISK borne by the operator), the operator recognises an <strong>INTANGIBLE ASSET</strong> — representing its RIGHT to charge users — rather than a receivable, since the ultimate amount to be collected isn't a fixed or determinable sum.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>The Receivable Model</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Used when the grantor unconditionally guarantees payment</div><div>Where the GRANTOR (rather than users) UNCONDITIONALLY GUARANTEES to pay the operator specified, determinable amounts (regardless of usage — e.g. fixed "availability payments" for keeping infrastructure available and operational), the operator recognises a <strong>RECEIVABLE</strong> instead — since the amount to be received is contractually fixed or determinable and not contingent on usage.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Revenue Recognition During the Construction/Upgrade Phase</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">ASC 606 governs construction/upgrade services revenue</div><div>During the CONSTRUCTION or UPGRADE phase, the operator recognises REVENUE for those construction/upgrade SERVICES under the normal ASC 606 revenue recognition model (generally over time, as the performance obligation to build/upgrade infrastructure is satisfied), with a corresponding intangible asset or receivable built up as consideration for that construction activity.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — the Intangible Asset Model (a Toll Road)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Demand risk borne by the operator</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Building and operating a toll road</div><div class="wk-body">An operator builds a toll road for a state government under a 30-year concession, earning revenue solely from tolls charged to drivers. The state controls toll pricing and retains ownership of the road at the concession's end.</div><div class="wk-answer">The operator recognises an <strong>INTANGIBLE ASSET</strong> — its right to charge tolls — measured initially based on the fair value of the construction services provided, since the ultimate amount the operator will collect depends entirely on future traffic volumes (genuine demand risk), not a fixed guaranteed sum from the state.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — the Receivable Model (Guaranteed Availability Payments)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A fixed, guaranteed payment stream</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Operating a water treatment facility under fixed availability payments</div><div class="wk-body">An operator builds and operates a water treatment facility for a municipality, receiving fixed annual "availability payments" from the municipality regardless of actual water volume treated, so long as the facility remains operational and meets contractual performance standards.</div><div class="wk-answer">Since the municipality (grantor) UNCONDITIONALLY GUARANTEES fixed, determinable payments — NOT contingent on usage — the operator recognises a <strong>RECEIVABLE</strong> for the construction services provided, rather than an intangible asset, reflecting the ABSENCE of genuine demand risk in this arrangement.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 853 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Description of the arrangement, including its significant terms and the nature of rights/obligations</span><span>Nature and extent of rights to use specified infrastructure, and obligations to provide/receive specified services</span><span>Changes in the arrangement during the period</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether both control indicators are met, bringing the arrangement into ASC 853's scope</span><span>Whether the arrangement should follow the intangible asset model or the receivable model, or a combination of both</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Fair value of construction/upgrade services provided, forming the basis for initial intangible asset or receivable measurement</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>ASC 853 vs IFRIC 12 — a Genuinely Converged Standard</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 853 vs IFRIC 12</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 853)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS (IFRIC 12)</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Scope control indicators</span><span>Grantor controls services/pricing and residual interest</span><span>Same two indicators — converged by deliberate design</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Intangible asset model (demand risk)</span><span>Available</span><span>Available — converged</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Financial asset/receivable model (guaranteed payment)</span><span>Available</span><span>Available — converged</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="note">ASC 853 was deliberately added to the Codification to mirror IFRIC 12 — this is one of the clearest examples of intentional, standard-level convergence anywhere in the library, alongside ASC 606/IFRS 15 and ASC 820/IFRS 13.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 853 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Construction/upgrade services revenue</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Recognised under ASC 606, generally over time</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Intangible asset model</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Right to charge users recognised as an intangible asset</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Receivable model</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Guaranteed payment right recognised as a receivable</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 606</span><span>Governs revenue recognition for the construction/upgrade services provided under a concession arrangement</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 350</span><span>Intangibles — governs subsequent amortisation of the intangible asset recognised under the intangible asset model</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 842</span><span>Applies instead if the arrangement meets the definition of a lease, excluding it from ASC 853's scope</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Grantor</div><div class="glossary-def">The public-sector (or public-sector-like) entity that grants the operator the right to operate infrastructure under a service concession arrangement.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Operator</div><div class="glossary-def">The private entity that builds, upgrades, operates, or maintains public infrastructure under a service concession arrangement.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Availability payment</div><div class="glossary-def">A fixed payment made by the grantor to the operator for keeping infrastructure available and operational, regardless of actual usage.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc853 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
